# Copy to .env and fill in real values. Never commit .env.

# ── Supabase (Auth + Postgres host) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Auth is handled by Supabase Auth; data is accessed directly via Drizzle.
# Get these from: Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Server-only (never expose to browser): used to create staff accounts, etc.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<service-role-key>

# ── Database connections ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Get these from: Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → Database → Connection string

# Runtime (app queries): pooled via Supavisor, port 6543.
# IMPORTANT (Vercel/serverless): use this IPv4 transaction-pooler host
# (…pooler.supabase.com:6543). Do NOT use the direct db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432
# endpoint: it is IPv6-only and Vercel functions are IPv4-only, so it cannot
# connect from production. The pooler also requires `prepare: false` (see db/index.ts).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true

# Migrations only (Drizzle Kit / startup script): direct connection, port 5432
DIRECT_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# ── App URL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Base URL of this app (same origin as the browser uses)
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://your-domain.example

# ── Admin ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated emails auto-promoted to super_admin on sign-in
SUPER_ADMIN_EMAILS=admin@example.com

# ── OAuth providers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Configure GitHub & Google in the Supabase dashboard (Auth → Providers).
# OAuth client id/secret live in Supabase, NOT here. Set each provider's
# callback URL to: https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback

# ── Judging: GitHub repo requirements check ───────────────────────────────────
# Personal access token (classic or fine-grained, read-only public repo scope)
# used by the step-1 repo check. Without it GitHub allows only 60 requests/hour,
# which will not cover a full field of teams. With it, 5000/hour.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ── Optional: email (Resend) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
RESEND_API_KEY=
# From address when using Resend (e.g. "App <onboarding@resend.dev>")
EMAIL_FROM=

# ── SEO ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Canonical public URL for SEO, sitemap, JSON-LD (production)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://your-domain.example

# ── TLS (local HTTPS) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set both to enable in-app HTTPS, omit for HTTP fallback
SSL_KEY_PATH=./certs/server.key
SSL_CERT_PATH=./certs/server.crt

On your question: no. A GITHUB_TOKEN isn't entropy you generate — GitHub issues it and validates it against its own records. A random hash returns 401 on every call, and the repo check would flag every team as failing. Create one at github.com/settings/tokens with public_repo scope (classic), or fine-grained → Public repositories → read-only.

What I changed, now that public repos are the rule:

┌──────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   File                   │                                                  Change                                                   │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ lib/judging/repo-check.ts:165            │ 404 message now names the fix — "must be public… ask the team to make it public, or to fix the GitHub     │
│                                          │ URL". GitHub returns 404 for both a private repo and a typo, so the wording covers both.                  │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ components/participant/AppUrlSection.tsx │ Hint under the GitHub URL field on the hour-one path                                                      │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ components/dashboard/ProjectSection.tsx  │ githubHint key × 3 locale blocks, rendered under the field                                                │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ lib/i18n.ts                              │ round1Goal + round1Bullet1 now say public GitHub repo, translated in en/de/es                             │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ .env.example                             │ States the token lifts the rate limit and does not unlock private repos — a broader scope won't change    │
│                                          │ that                                                                                                      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Verified: tsc --noEmit clean, 66/66 tests pass, build compiles, lint at 65 problems / 30 errors — identical to baseline.

Worth knowing: this wasn't only about the viewer I built. runRepoCheck already 404'd on private repos, so any team with a private repo would have been silently flagged as failing step 1 with nothing telling them why. The hint closes that.

skipped: a live repo-visibility check on save (would warn at submit time instead of judging night) — add it if teams still show up with private repos despite the hint.

Still on you: GITHUB_TOKEN is absent from .env.local. Nothing works against GitHub until it's set.